Okay, I will admit, the inability to wish for your own quest
artifact is slightly quirky.  I suspect the main reason for it
is storyline rather than gameplay.  Specifically, all the dialog
in the quest is written around your need to retrieve said object.
The *main* purpose of the quest, from a gameplay perspective,
is of course to get the silver bell.  The quest artifact is just a
bonus (and a much nicer bonus for some roles than others;
honestly, if you didn't need the bell for the invocation, would
you ever bother with the barbarian quest?)

One other quirky thing is the fact that certain characters
will steal your own quest artifact, but not others.  They
should steal any artifact you happen to be carrying, IMO
(possibly with preference for the invocation items and/or
the Amulet of Yendor).

> > I kind of feel that way about credit cloning and other
> > forms of dishonest shop exploitation.
>
> Well, I certainly don't know about your ethical concept here. For
> example, what would you do if your pet purple worm swallows the
> shopkeeper and give you free access to everything, items and gold.

By the time you have pets powerful enough to kill shopkeepers,
gold is generally a non-issue anyhow due to the overwhelming
quantities of it that appear on lower dungeon levels.

As for killing being at least as unethical as stealing, well,
yes, in real life, but if you don't accept killing as part of
the game, NetHack would be kind of unplayable.  That
would be rather like playing Diplomacy without lying.

Aside from that, shopkeepers have a fixed monster
difficulty (personally, I would like to see ones at deeper
dungeon levels be more advanced, but that would be
a change; even then, any given shopkeeper would have
a particular monster difficulty), so dispatching them is
something you have to reach a certain point in the
game to be able to do -- an obstacle to be overcome,
like most everything in NetHack.  Credit cloning the
weapons shop on DL2 with your little dog for three
thousand zorkmids just seems immeasurably more
like circumventing the limits of the developers' ability
to program a reasonable shopkeeper AI.

